{
  "id": "Compound::DB00860 - Gene::2908",
  "verbalization": "Prednisolone is linked to Nr3c1 through a shared pathway involving corticosteroids and their receptor interactions. The evidence suggests that Prednisolone, through its inclusion in corticosteroid hormone receptor agonists, binds to Nr3c1, as seen in the case of Alclometasone and Medrysone. These corticosteroids, which are part of the broader class of therapeutic glucocorticoids, are known to interact with the glucocorticoid receptor, a protein that regulates gene expression. The presence of a common ancestor, 21-Hydroxy Steroid, in all the paths reinforces the notion that Prednisolone and its analogs are part of a broader class of corticosteroids that share a common metabolic pathway. The relationship between Prednisolone and Nr3c1 is thus mediated through the glucocorticoid receptor, with the binding of corticosteroids to Nr3c1 being a key mechanism in their biological effects. The evidence from the three paths collectively supports the idea that Prednisolone exerts its effects via a pathway that involves the glucocorticoid receptor and Nr3c1, highlighting the importance of corticosteroid receptor interactions in drug action.",
